0 July 2024"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2189"} "National Shooting Centre, Châteauroux, France"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2013117"} "30 from 24 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The men’s trap shooting event at the 2024 Summer Olympics took place on July 29 and 30 at the " [:a {:href "/venues/2189"} "National Shooting Centre"] ", in Châteauroux, France. The competition featured 30 shooters from 24 nations."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the qualification round, four men scored 123 out of 125 targets to qualify for the final, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/154095"} "Qi Ying"] " securing the top qualifying spot after a shoot-off. Two others tied on 122 reached the final after a further shoot-off."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final was contested under intense heat, giving an incentive to those competitors who had the necessary focus and endurance. World record holder " [:a {:href "/athletes/154127"} "Nathan Hales"] " of Great Britain displayed remarkable composure, hitting 48 out of 50 targets in the final, setting a new Olympic record and clinching the gold medal.  Asian Games champion Qi Ying secured the silver with 44 hits, whilst Guatemala’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/123253"} "Jean-Pierre Brol"] ", at 41 years old, earned the bronze with 35 hits, marking only the second Olympic medal in his country’s history."] "\n\n" [:p {} "This victory marked Great Britain’s first Olympic shooting gold since 2012. Hales credited his success to collaboration with 2012 double trap gold medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/122671"} "Peter Wilson"] ", stating “It’s absolutely a dream come true. I worked with Peter over the past couple of years which has been great help as well.”"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Neither reigning Olympic champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/125224"} "Jiří Lipták"] " nor World champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/143190"} "Matthew Coward-Holley"] " got close to reaching th